@prefix ds: <https://ds.canonical.com/>.
@prefix anatomy: <https://anatomy.canonical.com/>.
@prefix dt: <https://dt.canonical.com/>.

ds:apps_anbox.component.theme_switcher a ds:Component;
    ds:name "ThemeSwitcher";
    ds:summary "A control used to switch the interface theme between light and dark modes. It helps users quickly choose the visual appearance that is most comfortable for their environment, preference, or accessibility needs. Use it when the product supports multiple visual themes and users may need an explicit, persistent way to change them.\n\n";
    ds:anatomyDsl "@prefix ds: <https://ds.canonical.com/> .\n\nds:global.component.theme_switcher a ds:Component ;  \n    ds:name \"Theme switcher\" ;  \n    ds:description \"Control that lets users switch the interface theme between light and dark modes, typically represented with sun and moon visuals.\" ;  \n    ds:tier ds:global ;  \n    ds:whenToUse \"When a product supports multiple themes and users need a quick, explicit way to change the interface appearance.\" ;  \n    ds:whenNotToUse \"When the product supports only one theme, or when theme changes are controlled automatically without user override.\" ;  \n    ds:guidelines \"Use clear sun and moon affordances, preserve the user's selection, provide accessible labels and keyboard support, and communicate the current state clearly to assistive technologies.\" .";
    ds:anatomyClassic "The theme switcher is composed of a small set of visual and interactive elements that communicate state and afford interaction clearly.\n\n### Parts\n\n- **Container**\n  - The clickable/tappable area that wraps the entire control.\n  - Defines hit area, spacing, and layout.\n  - Appears as an icon highlighting the current state of the switcher.\n- **Toggle / Button**\n  - The main interactive element.\n  - Handles user input (click, tap, keyboard).\n  - Visually represents the current state.\n- **Icon (light)**\n  - Typically a sun symbol.\n  - Represents the light theme state.\n  - May be shown when active or as part of a transition.\n- **Icon (dark)**\n  - Typically a moon symbol.\n  - Represents the dark theme state.\n  - May replace or animate from the light icon.\n- **Focus indicator**\n  - Visible outline or highlight when the component receives keyboard focus.\n  - Must be clearly visible in both light and dark themes.\n- **Label (optional)**\n  - Text describing the control (e.g., “Light”, “Dark”, “Theme”).\n  - Can be visible or accessible-only.\n\n\n\n### States\n\n- **Default**\n  - Shows current theme (sun or moon).\n- **Hover**\n  - Indicates interactivity on pointer devices.\n- **Active / Pressed**\n  - Brief feedback when user activates the control.\n- **Focused**\n  - Visible focus ring for keyboard navigation.\n- **Selected (Light)**\n  - Light theme is active.\n- **Selected (Dark)**\n  - Dark theme is active.\n- **Disabled**\n  - Non-interactive state when switching is unavailable.";
    ds:usage "## When to use\n\nUse this component when users need a direct way to switch between supported visual themes.\n\nRecommended usage scenarios:\n\n- **User preference override**  \nUse the theme switcher when users should be able to choose light or dark mode regardless of the system default.\n- **Long-running applications**  \nUse it in products users keep open for long periods, where lighting conditions may change during use.\n- **Accessibility and comfort**  \nUse it when darker or lighter interfaces may improve readability, reduce glare, or better fit user comfort.\n- **Global application setting**  \nUse it when theme is a product-level preference that should affect the full interface consistently.\n\n## When not to use\n\nAvoid using this component when changing theme is not a real user choice or when it affects too small a scope.\n\nAvoid usage:\n\n- **Single-theme products**  \nDo not show a theme switcher if only one visual theme is available.\n- **Temporary visual effects**  \nDo not use it for controls that only change a local surface, card, or panel rather than the product theme.\n- **Unclear or decorative toggles**  \nAvoid using icon-only controls without an accessible name or without a clear indication of the current state.\n- **Automatic-only behavior**  \nAvoid showing it if the theme is always inherited from system settings and the user cannot override it.";
    ds:guidelines "## **Accessibility**\n\n### **Accessible name**\n\nThe component must expose a clear accessible name such as “Toggle theme”, “Switch to dark mode”, or “Switch to light mode”.\n\n### **State communication**\n\nAssistive technologies must be able to understand the current state. If implemented as a toggle, expose whether it is on or off in a way that maps clearly to the selected theme.\n\n### **Contrast**\n\nEnsure the control, icons, focus indicator, and any labels remain visible and meet contrast requirements in both light and dark themes.\n\n### **Target size**\n\nThe interactive area should be large enough for pointer and touch interaction, not just the visible icon.\n\n## Device specification\n\n### **Desktop**\n\nPlace the theme switcher in a predictable global location, such as the top navigation bar, settings area, or profile/preferences menu.\n\n### **Mobile**\n\nUse a touch-friendly target and avoid placing the control too close to other global actions.";
    ds:tier ds:apps_anbox.
